What is Index in SQL?
========================

An Index in SQL is a database object that helps to find data faster from a table.

Normally, if there is no index, database checks every row one by one if the table has large data like thousands or millions of 
rows which makes searching slow to overcome this problem we use index.
An index helps the database to directly go to the required data, without checking all rows.

This is called full table scan.
It is slow when table is big.

Full table scan is slow, especially when the table has large data like thousands or millions of rows.


Index works like a book index page.
If you want one topic, you go directly to page number.
You don’t read full book.


| Object Type | Object Name |
| ----------- | ----------- |
| Table       | users       |
| Index       | idx_email   |
| View        | user_view   |


Why Index is Important?
------------------------

Indexes are very important in real projects.

Indexes help to:

Speed up SELECT queries (name..)

Speed up JOIN queries (on o.user_id = c.id)

Speed up WHERE condition (where email = "abc@gmail")

Speed up ORDER BY (created_at and updated_at)

Indexes reduce disk reading.
Less disk read means better performance.

Indexes are very useful when table has large data like lakhs or millions of rows.

How Index Works Internally (Simple)
-----------------------------------
Index stores:

Column value

Row address

Index data is stored in sorted order.

Database uses B-Tree structure.

B-Tree helps database to:

Divide data

Search step by step

Reach data quickly

Instead of checking 1 lakh rows,
database checks only few steps.


Disadvantages of Index
------------------------------
Indexes also have some problems.

INSERT becomes slow

UPDATE becomes slow

DELETE becomes slow

Index needs extra storage





Ex : 
-->CREATE INDEX idx_email ON users(email);


--> module.exports = (sequelize, DataTypes) => {
  const User = sequelize.define(
    "User",
    {
      email: {
        type: DataTypes.STRING
      },
      description: {
        type: DataTypes.TEXT
      }
    },
    {
      tableName: "users",
      indexes: [
        {
          name: "idx_email",
          fields: ["email"]
        },
        {
          name: "idx_description",
          fields: ["description"]
        }
      ]
    }
  );

  return User;
};


